{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.62,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["C1"],
      "pins": ["C1.1", "C1.2"],
      "nets": ["Display_Power_Switch", "GND"],
      "observed": "On sheet /EPD_Driver/ the capacitor C1 sits directly on the display power rail (Display_Power_Switch to GND, next to C2) and is drawn with the value 0.1 uF / 16 V.",
      "expected": "This capacitor is one of the Pervasive-Displays reference-circuit decoupling capacitors that the specification lists as 1 uF / 25 V; it should be 1 uF / 25 V, not 0.1 uF / 16 V.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "\"C1\" / \"0.1uF/16V\" on the Display_Power_Switch rail beside \"C2\" / \"1uF/6.3V\" (sheet EPD_Driver, tile 1, left of L1 10uH)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "| C2 default: 1 µF/25 V | C1/C5/C7/C8/C9/C10 = 1 µF/25 V | Matches default |"}
      ],
      "impact": "The EPD power rail that feeds the panel VDD/VDDIO pins and the boost inductor L1 is now bypassed with a capacitor ten times smaller (and rated 16 V instead of 25 V). The rail is less well decoupled against the boost converter's switching edges, which can degrade the e-paper drive waveforms, and the lower voltage rating reduces margin; the board would still power up, but with different (worse) display-rail noise behaviour than intended.",
      "confidence": 0.62
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design is largely consistent with the specification, but the EPD driver sheet's input decoupling capacitor C1 is populated as 0.1 uF/16 V instead of the specified 1 uF/25 V (the only stated passive value that disagrees with the drawing), which is a sourcing/value deviation on the display power rail."
}

